.nh
.TH "UNIQX" "1" "Jan 2024" "gnux" "gnux manual"

.SH NAME
uniqx - Report or omit repeated lines.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBuniqx [OPTION]... [INPUT [OUTPUT]]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
uniqx filters adjacent matching lines from INPUT (or standard input), writing to OUTPUT (or standard output). With no options, matching lines are merged to the first occurrence. Only adjacent lines are compared, so a file is usually sorted first. -c prefixes each line with its number of occurrences, -d prints one copy of each repeated line, -u only the lines that are not repeated, and -D every line of each repeated group, with --all-repeated=METHOD (none, prepend, separate) putting an empty line before or between the groups. --group=METHOD
\[la]separate, prepend, append, both\[ra] prints every line with the groups delimited by an empty line, and cannot be combined with -c, -d, -D or -u; -D cannot be combined with -c either. The comparison skips the first N fields with -f (a field is a run of blanks then non-blanks), then N characters with -s, and compares at most N characters with -w. -z makes NUL the line delimiter, and the output always ends each line with it. Deviations from GNU: characters for -s and -w are bytes, as in GNU's C locale, and -i folds ASCII letters only. The obsolete -N and +N forms of -f and -s are not accepted. METHOD arguments may be abbreviated, as in GNU.

.PP
EXIT STATUS.
  0  success: every operand was processed
  1  failure: an operand could not be processed (diagnosed on standard error), or a command-line mistake

.PP
HELP. uniqx --help shows the options and the first examples; gnux help uniqx prints the full manual, and gnux help uniqx OPTION explains one option.

.PP
SEE ALSO. cutx, wcx, catx, headx.

.PP
PERMISSIONS. Needs read permission on INPUT and search permission on the directories leading to it. When OUTPUT is given it is created or truncated, which needs write permission on it if it exists, or on its directory if it does not. INPUT itself is never modified.

.PP
USE CASES.
  - Removing duplicate lines from sorted output.
  - Counting how often each value occurs with sort and -c, a frequency table.
  - Listing only the values that appear more than once with -d.
  - Finding the values that appear exactly once with -u.
  - Grouping related lines visually with --group.
  - Comparing lines while ignoring a leading timestamp or ID with -f or -s.
  - Treating lines as equal when they share a prefix with -w.
  - Deduplicating NUL-separated file lists safely with -z.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-D\fP, \fB--all-repeated\fP[=""]
	print all duplicate lines; groups can be delimited with an empty line; \fBMETHOD\fR is none (the default), prepend or separate

.PP
\fB-w\fP, \fB--check-chars\fP=""
	compare no more than \fBN\fR characters in lines

.PP
\fB-c\fP, \fB--count\fP[=false]
	prefix lines by the number of occurrences

.PP
\fB--group\fP[=""]
	show all items, separating groups with an empty line; \fBMETHOD\fR is separate (the default), prepend, append or both

.PP
\fB--help\fP[=false]
	display this help and exit

.PP
\fB-i\fP, \fB--ignore-case\fP[=false]
	ignore differences in case when comparing

.PP
\fB-d\fP, \fB--repeated\fP[=false]
	only print duplicate lines, one for each group

.PP
\fB-s\fP, \fB--skip-chars\fP=""
	avoid comparing the first \fBN\fR characters

.PP
\fB-f\fP, \fB--skip-fields\fP=""
	avoid comparing the first \fBN\fR fields

.PP
\fB-u\fP, \fB--unique\fP[=false]
	only print unique lines

.PP
\fB--version\fP[=false]
	output version information and exit

.PP
\fB-z\fP, \fB--zero-terminated\fP[=false]
	line delimiter is NUL, not newline


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Remove adjacent duplicate lines
  uniqx names.txt

  # Write the result to another file
  uniqx names.txt unique-names.txt

  # Count the occurrences of each line
  uniqx -c sorted.txt

  # Print only the lines that are repeated
  uniqx -d sorted.txt

  # Print only the lines that are not repeated
  uniqx -u sorted.txt

  # Print every repeated line, the groups separated by an empty line
  uniqx --all-repeated=separate sorted.txt

  # Print all lines with each group of equal lines set apart
  uniqx --group sorted.txt

  # Compare without regard to case
  uniqx -i words.txt

  # Ignore the first field (a timestamp, say) when comparing
  uniqx -f 1 events.log

  # Ignore the first 4 characters when comparing
  uniqx -s 4 ids.txt

  # Treat lines that share their first 8 characters as equal
  uniqx -w 8 commits.txt

  # Deduplicate a NUL-separated list
  uniqx -z files.lst

  # Remove duplicates from a pipeline's output
  catx a.txt b.txt | uniqx

  # Count the distinct lines of a file
  uniqx sorted.txt | wcx -l

  # Deduplicate in the portable gnux shell
  gnux shell -c 'catx sorted.txt | uniqx -c'
.EE
